3. NOTICES CARRIED FOR THE PROVIDERS
------------------------------------

3.1 Accuracy. Model outputs in this package may be inaccurate, and some of them
    are: the study measures behaviour, and a recorded output being wrong is
    often the finding. Nothing here should be relied on without independently
    confirming it against the artifact it came from. (Anthropic Commercial Terms
    section D.3.)

3.2 AI generation. Every transcript, label, verdict and adjudication in this
    package was generated by an AI model, not by a human, except where a file
    says otherwise in its own text. (OpenAI terms; the same disclosure answers
    Google's not-solely-human rule.)

3.3 Per-item model attribution. Every validation label and judge verdict is
    stored in a file named for the model that produced it, and the run
    configuration that produced it is committed beside it. (Satisfies Z.ai's
    AI-marking duty, Moonshot's mark-preservation rule, and the attribution
    xAI's consumer terms ask for on the Grok CLI surface.)

3.4 Z.ai reuse restriction, verbatim as PROVIDER_TERMS.md collected it:
    "Z.ai's API terms prohibit customers from using GLM outputs for
    external-model development, training or labeling; the GLM-5.3 labels in
    these tables were used for instrument validation only and must not be
    reused by Z.ai customers for those purposes."

3.5 Cohere output restriction. Cohere's SaaS Agreement section 2(d)(ii)(iii)
    bars its customers from using Output "for the purpose of benchmarking or
    conducting competitive analysis" of the Services or Cohere Products; the
    Cohere Terms of Use repeat that bar and add one on distributing Content
    received from the Cohere Solution. The command-a answers in this package
    were obtained over OpenRouter, and OpenRouter -- not this study -- was
    Cohere's customer: no Cohere API key appears in this repository and
    BUDGET.md carries no Cohere line. Section 4.2 excepts the per-item answer
    files from both grants and withholds both limbs: it permits reading, local
    copying and processing with the study's committed code, and it grants no
    right to publish, market, advertise or otherwise distribute those files, and
    none to benchmark or conduct competitive analysis. Nothing in this package
    purports to license anyone for the uses those clauses name. Whether Cohere's
    customer-facing bars reach a downstream OpenRouter user is an open reading,
    as PROVIDER_TERMS.md records; nothing here rests on the answer.


